                "text": "**Defining the Transformations**\n\nI've established the transformations: rotate 180 degrees for the top-left quadrant, vertical flip for the top-right, horizontal flip for the bottom-left, and the original input for the bottom-right. The input grid is 8x8, and the output will be 16x16. I am now confirming these definitions.\n\n\n**Validating the Logic**\n\nI've tested the transformations with a 2x2 matrix and the output. Specifically, I've confirmed that concatenating the rotated and vertically flipped versions of the input forms the top half, and the horizontally flipped and original input forms the bottom. I realized the bottom half is also a vertical flip of the top half. Furthermore, each row of the top half is the concatenation of a row from the rotated and vertically flipped inputs. The key insight is that, for any given row, the vertically flipped portion is the reverse of the rotated portion.\n\n\n**Refining the Row Construction**\n\nI've made a key breakthrough in how to generate each row of the top half of the output. I've realized the reversed portion of each rotated row is identical to a row in the original input. Specifically, I realized that for a given row in the rotated input, its reverse corresponds to a row in the original input based on row index. This is a crucial simplification and should make the final solution easier to implement.\n\n\n",
